Title: Static Overloading — Koka Feature Guide
[INCLUDE=../sidebar-sub.mdk]

# Static Overloading { .heading-no-number }

Koka supports _static overloading_: multiple functions (or values) can share the same name,
and the compiler uses type information to pick the right one at every call site.
This is done entirely through _qualified names_ — there are no type classes or
instances involved.

---

## How It Works: Qualified Names

Every definition in Koka has a _fully qualified name_ built from its module path:

```unchecked
std/core/int/show     // the show function for int
std/core/list/map     // the map function for lists
```

When you write a plain name like `show`, the compiler searches all in-scope modules
and uses the argument types to pick the right one. You can always spell out the
full qualifier when you need to be explicit:

```koka
fun example-explicit()
  [1,2].std/core/list/map(std/core/int/show)
```

Or use a shorter partial qualifier:

```koka
fun example-semi-explicit()
  [1,2].list/map(core/int/show)
```

In practice, you almost never need to write qualifiers by hand — the compiler
resolves them for you, and IDEs show the resolved names as inlay hints
(`Ctrl+Alt` in VS Code).

---

## Defining Overloaded Functions

You can define multiple top-level functions with the same short name in the same module
using _local qualification_: prefix the function name with a type name and a `/`:

```koka
fun int/foo(i : int) : int
  i + 1

fun string/foo(s : string) : int
  s.count
```

Both are called `foo` by their unqualified name. The compiler picks between
them based on the argument type:

```koka
pub fun main()
  println(foo(41))           // resolves to int/foo
  println(foo("hello"))      // resolves to string/foo
```

---

## Type-Directed Disambiguation

Koka uses the _types of arguments_ to disambiguate overloaded names automatically.
No type annotations at the call site are usually needed:

```koka
fun example3()
  foo(11) + foo("hi there")
```

When the argument type is known, the right `foo` is selected statically.

### When Disambiguation Fails

If the compiler cannot infer enough type information, it reports an ambiguity.
You can resolve this by adding a type annotation:

```koka
fun example4a(x : int)   // annotation on x resolves foo(x) to int/foo
  foo(x) + foo("hi there")
```

Or by writing a partial qualifier explicitly:

```koka
fun example4b(x)
  int/foo(x) + foo("hi there")  // int/foo is explicit; the other is inferred
```

---

## Why Not Type Classes?

Koka's overloading is simpler than Haskell-style type classes in one important way:
a Koka program with fully qualified names has an **untyped dynamic semantics** —
you can run it without knowing any types at all.

This is not true for type classes. Consider `show []` in Haskell: the _behaviour_
changes depending on the type of `[]` (e.g. `[Char]` vs `[Int]`), so you cannot
execute it without type information. In Koka, which `show` is called is encoded
directly in the fully-qualified name, so overloading is purely a _convenience_
on top of exact disambiguation.

---

## Combining Overloading with Implicits

Static overloading is often combined with _implicit parameters_ to achieve
flexible ad-hoc polymorphism similar to type classes but without the machinery.
[Read about implicits](implicits.html){.learn}

